// Add lines like this to spawn processes at startup.
// Note that running niri as a session supports xdg-desktop-autostart,
// which may be more convenient to use.
// See the binds section below for more spawn examples.

// Noctalia is the bar/shell (replaces waybar). Install: sudo dnf install noctalia
// Started by noctalia.service instead of spawn-at-startup, so systemd can
// restart it when the OOM killer takes it out. The unit is WantedBy=niri.service.
// spawn-at-startup "noctalia"
// waybar disabled on purpose — Noctalia provides the bar. Do NOT run both.
// spawn-at-startup "waybar"

// nm-applet is NOT started here, and deliberately has no systemd unit either.
// It arrives via /etc/xdg/autostart/nm-applet.desktop, whose NotShowIn only
// covers KDE and GNOME -- niri is neither, so it autostarts. That is the right
// mechanism: nm-applet forks, so a unit reports "inactive" while the process is
// still running and Restart= supervises nothing.
//
// It is required, not optional. The wifi connections use psk-flags=1, so the PSK
// lives in gnome-keyring and NM will not connect until an agent hands it over.
// Noctalia registers org.noctalia.SecretAgent and looks like it covers this, but
// its GetSecrets only ever prompts and its SaveSecrets is an empty stub -- so it
// cannot auto-connect at boot and silently discards passwords NM asks it to save.
// If nm-applet is ever removed, wifi stops persisting.
//
// Post-login only either way: the keyring is locked until you authenticate.

// To run a shell command (with variables, pipes, etc.), use spawn-sh-at-startup:
// spawn-sh-at-startup "qs -c ~/source/qs/MyAwesomeShell"
